<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext traffic policy.

  Background (measured conclusion, not speculation): CEF pages come from two sources —
    1. Local-resource virtual host http://cef/..., served by CefResourceProvider;
    2. Page URLs pushed by the server; in the SA-MP ecosystem the vast majority are http://<IP>:<port>.
  We once narrowed the allowlist to "cef / localhost / 127.0.0.1", and a real-device E2E run immediately failed with:

      CefWebView: browser 8 onReceivedError http://192.168.0.117:8000/index.html
                  : -1 net::ERR_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED

  In other words, narrowing it blocks the entire second category of sources — and players running their own servers cannot be expected to set up
  HTTPS certificates and a domain just for this client. Domain rules also do not support IP-range wildcards, so there is no way to "allow only the LAN".

  So cleartext is allowed wholesale here. The real boundary does not rely on this layer: CEF only loads URLs pushed by the server,
  and does not accept arbitrary navigation inside pages; if this ever needs tightening, this file is the place to do it (per-domain allowlist),
  rather than going back to a global ban.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
